# Whether anybody looked for a dependence, the other half of the couplings. One row per filing.
#
# The couplings and the search are one pair
#   The couplings and their search are filed as a pair, and emitting one of them is the failure this
#   model exists to refuse. As entries/couplings.sqlc says, a filing with no couplings has either a
#   stack of independent layers or nobody who looked, and those are opposite claims. Most documents
#   on the corpus state no coupling, so a diagram drawing only the couplings is silent about them in
#   a way a reader will take as independence.
#
# A diagram is the worst place for that
#   An absence is invisible on a page in a way it is not in a table. A missing row can be counted; a
#   missing line cannot be seen. That is what `examples/diagramming` itself demands, that what
#   could not be drawn must be listed, applied to the corpus instead of to the notation.
#
# The typed answer, not the note
#   The four answers are the fact: `unmeasured` is nobody looked, `none` is somebody looked and
#   found nothing, `notApplicable` is there is no pair to couple, and a NULL is somebody looked and
#   the couplings are the ones drawn. The note is the argument for the answer, it is free prose, and
#   diagrams/dependences.sqlc says why prose does not travel into an artifact with no unit on it.
#
# Carried on the lane set
#   The answer goes in the lane set's own documentation, because the lane set is the division the
#   search is about.
-- pm:Stack/pm:couplings/pm:absent; carried as the laneSet's own documentation.
SELECT s.filing,
       coalesce(s.answer::text, 'stated') AS answer
FROM (
    :compose(epistemics/coupling_searches.sqlc)
) s
